L268: ## 5 Theoretical analysis
L269: ### 5.1 Step Size Stability
L270: 
L271: To ensure that the proposed causality-guided diffusion policy is numerically and theoretically feasible, we analyze its dynamics under the stochastic differential equation (SDE) formulation. Specifically, we will show that the causality-guided diffusion model does not exhibit unbounded step sizes.
L272: 
L273: A common diffusion model can be represented as a SDE (cite58†Song et al., 2020b ). The forward term for the action $a_{t}$ can be written as:
L274:  | $$da_{t}=f\left(a_{t},t\right)dt+g(t)d\bar{w}_{t}$$  |
L275: 
L276: where $f\left(a_{t},t\right)=-\frac{1}{2}\beta(t)\left(a_{t}-\mu_{\theta}\left(a_{t},t\right)\right)$, with $\mu_{\theta}(a_{t},t)$ denoting a parameterized mean with parameters $\theta$, $d\bar{w}_{t}$ is a Wiener process (Brownian motion), and $g(t)=\sqrt{\beta(t)}$. The reverse process corresponds to denoising can be written as:
L277:  | $$da_{t}=\left[f\left(a_{t},t\right)-g(t)^{2}\nabla_{a_{t}}\log p_{t}\left(a_{t}\right)\right]dt+g(t)d\bar{w},$$  |
L278: 
L279: where $p_{t}\left(a_{t}\right)$ is the sampling distribution.
L280: 
L281: Under our causality-guided diffusion, the SDE of the diffusion process is:
L282:  | $\displaystyle da_{t}=[f$  | $\displaystyle\left(a_{t},t\right)-g(t)^{2}\nabla_{a_{t}}\log p_{t}\left(a_{t}\right)$  |  | (9)
L283:  |  | $\displaystyle-\gamma_{t}\nabla_{a_{t}}\log p_{\varphi}\left(s_{t+1}\mid s_{t},\operatorname{\textbf{do}}\left(a_{t}\right)\right)$  |
L284:  |  | $\displaystyle\left.-\beta_{t}\nabla_{a_{t}}\log p_{\omega}\left(r^{*}\mid s_{t+1},\operatorname{\textbf{do}}\left(a_{t}\right)\right)\right]dt+g(t)d\bar{w}_{t}.$  |
L285: Under the SDE formulation of the causality-guided diffusion policy, we introduce a set of basic and mild assumptions in Assumption cite88†1 to ensure that the causality-guided diffusion policy is well-defined and valid.
L286: ###### Assumption 1 (Lipschitz Regularity of the causality guided Drift).
L287: 
L288: There exist constants $L_{f},L_{s},L_{\varphi},L_{\omega}\geq 0$ such that for any $a,a^{\prime}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}$ and any time $t\in[0,T]$, the following Lipschitz conditions hold:
L289:  |  | $\displaystyle\|f(a,t)-f(a^{\prime},t)\|\leq L_{f}\|a-a^{\prime}\|,$  |  | (A1)
L290:  |  | $\displaystyle\|\nabla_{a}\log p_{t}(a)-\nabla_{a^{\prime}}\log p_{t}(a^{\prime})\|\leq L_{s}\|a-a^{\prime}\|,$  |  | (A2)
L291:  |  | $\displaystyle\|\nabla_{a}\log p_{\varphi}(\cdot\mid\operatorname{\textbf{do}}(a))-\nabla_{a^{\prime}}\log p_{\varphi}(\cdot\mid\operatorname{\textbf{do}}(a^{\prime}))\|\leq L_{\varphi}\|a-a^{\prime}\|,$  |  | (A3)
L292:  |  | $\displaystyle\|\nabla_{a}\log p_{\omega}(\cdot\mid\operatorname{\textbf{do}}(a))-\nabla_{a^{\prime}}\log p_{\omega}(\cdot\mid\operatorname{\textbf{do}}(a^{\prime}))\|\leq L_{\omega}\|a-a^{\prime}\|$  |  | (A4)
L293: In other words, the base drift term $f(a,t)$, the diffusion score $\nabla_{a}\log p_{t}(a)$, and the causal guidance terms $\nabla_{a}\log p_{\varphi}$ and $\nabla_{a}\log p_{\omega}$ are all globally Lipschitz continuous in $a$ with respective Lipschitz constants $L_{f}$, $L_{s}$, and $L_{\varphi}$.
L294: Assumptions cite89†A1 and cite90†A2 are the standard Lipschitz continuity and linear growth conditions for the drift term of an SDE (cite91†Oksendal, 2013 ; cite92†Karatzas and Shreve, 2014 ). These two assumptions guarantee the existence and uniqueness of the SDE solution. In our method, this implies that the action trajectory generated by the diffusion policy is also well-defined and unique. This matches the RL setting, where the agent cannot execute two different actions at the same time at a given state.
L295: Similarly, in Assumptions cite93†A3 and cite94†A4 , the two guidance terms are introduced into the diffusion model in a way that ensures the resulting SDE remains mathematically well-posed and numerically stable (cite57†Song and Ermon, 2019 ; cite95†Oh et al., 2024 ). These two mild assumptions ensure that the RL agent does not generate multiple inconsistent actions for the same state; otherwise, the policy may become undefined.
L296: Based on these assumptions, we define a sum Lipschitz factor as $L_{\text{sum }}(t)=L_{f}+g(t)^{2}L_{s}+\left|\gamma_{t}\right|L_{\varphi}+\left|\beta_{t}\right|L_{\omega}$. Then, we can have the following proposition:
L297: ###### Proposition 1.
L298: 
L299: Consider the causal-guided reverse diffusion SDE (cite96†9 ) under Assumption cite88†1 , the explicit Euler discretizations $a_{n+1}=a_{n}+b_{\text{guided }}\left(a_{n},t_{n}\right)\Delta t+g\left(t_{n}\right)\Delta\bar{w}_{n}$ is stable as the time step $\Delta t$ satisfies $\Delta t\leq\frac{\delta}{L_{\text{sum }}\left(t_{n}\right)}$ for some $0<\delta<1$. Equivalently, a sufficient stability constraint is
L300:  | $$\boxed{\Delta t\leq\frac{\delta}{L_{f}+g(t)^{2}L_{s}+\left|\gamma_{t}\right|L_{\varphi}+\left|\beta_{t}\right|L_{\varphi}}}.$$  |
L301: 
L302: Under this step-size condition, the Euler solution remains stable and the discretization error stays bounded.
L303: 
L304: Proposition cite97†1 guarantees that introducing causality-guidance into diffusion policies does not compromise the underlying diffusion model, while ensuring that the resulting SDE remains stable under reasonable step sizes.
L305: ###### Proof of Proposition 1.
L306: 
L307: We now analyze stability under the (explicit) Euler method. The drifted causility-guided term is dnoted as :
L308:  | $\displaystyle b_{\text{cg}}(a_{t},t)={}$  | $\displaystyle f(a_{t},t)-g(t)^{2}\,\nabla_{a_{t}}\log p_{t}(a_{t})$  |
L309:  |  | $\displaystyle-\gamma_{t}\,\nabla_{a_{t}}\log p_{\varphi}\!\left(s_{t+1}\mid s_{t},\operatorname{\mathbf{do}}(a_{t})\right)$  |
L310:  |  | $\displaystyle-\beta_{t}\,\nabla_{a_{t}}\log p_{\omega}\!\left(r^{*}\mid s_{t+1},\operatorname{\mathbf{do}}(a_{t})\right)$  |
L311: In the Euler method, the numerical approximation at each step is discretized as $t\to t_{n}$ as $t_{n}=n\Delta t,\quad n=0,1,\ldots,N$ by $\Delta t$ gap for each discretized gap. Then, the SDE is discretized as:
L312: 
L313:  | $$a_{n+1}=a_{n}+b_{\text{cg}}\left(a_{n},t_{n}\right)\Delta t+g\left(t_{n}\right)\Delta\bar{w}_{n}$$  |
L314: 
L315: where $\Delta\bar{w}_{n}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\Delta t\mathbf{I})$ and $t_{n+1}=t_{n}+\Delta t$. According to the exact solution expansion:
L316:  | $$a\left(t_{n+1}\right)=a\left(t_{n}\right)+b_{\text{cg}}\left(a\left(t_{n}\right),t_{n}\right)\Delta t+R_{n}+g\left(t_{n}\right)\Delta\bar{w}_{n}$$  |
L317: 
L318: where $R_{n}$ is the local truncation (remainder) error, the error between the true and numerical solutions can then be written as $e_{n}=a\left(t_{n}\right)-a_{n}$and it satisfies the following recursion:
L319: 
L320:  | $$e_{n+1}=e_{n}+\left(b_{\mathrm{cg}}\left(a\left(t_{n}\right),t_{n}\right)-b_{\mathrm{cg}}\left(a_{n},t_{n}\right)\right)\Delta t+R_{n}$$  |
L321: By Assumption cite18†5.1 , , when $n=0$, we can have $\left\|b_{\mathrm{cg}}\left(a\left(t_{0}\right),t_{0}\right)-b_{\mathrm{cg}}\left(a_{0},t_{0}\right)\right\|\leq L_{\text{sum }}\left(t_{0}\right)\left\|a\left(t_{0}\right)-a_{0}\right\|$, so $\left\|e_{1}\right\|\leq\left(1+\Delta tL_{\mathrm{sum}}(t_{0})\right)\left\|e_{0}\right\|+\left\|R_{0}\right\|$. This can be deduced for $m$ when $1<m<N$ as:
L322:  | $$\left\|e_{m}\right\|\leq\left(\prod_{k=0}^{m-1}\left(1+\Delta tL_{\mathrm{sum}}(t_{k})\right)\right)\left\|e_{0}\right\|+\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}\left(\prod_{k=j+1}^{m-1}\left(1+\Delta tL_{\mathrm{sum}}(t_{k})\right)\right)\left\|R_{j}\right\|$$  |
L323: 
L324: which gives us the recursion:
L325:  | $\displaystyle\left\|e_{m+1}\right\|$  | $\displaystyle\leq\left(1+\Delta tL_{m}\right)\left[\left(\prod_{k=0}^{m-1}\left(1+\Delta tL_{\mathrm{sum}}(t_{k})\right)\right)\left\|e_{0}\right\|+\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}\left(\prod_{k=j+1}^{m-1}\left(1+\Delta tL_{\mathrm{sum}}(t_{k})\right)\right)\left\|R_{j}\right\|\right]+\left\|R_{m}\right\|$  |
L326:  |  | $\displaystyle=\left(\prod_{k=0}^{m}\left(1+\Delta tL_{\mathrm{sum}}(t_{k})\right)\right)\left\|e_{0}\right\|+\sum_{j=0}^{m-1}\left(\prod_{k=j+1}^{m}\left(1+\Delta tL_{\mathrm{sum}}(t_{k})\right)\right)\left\|R_{j}\right\|+\left\|R_{m}\right\|$  |
L327: Finally, the error at step $N$ can be bounded as:
L328: 
L329:  | $$\left\|e_{N}\right\|\leq\left(\prod_{k=0}^{N-1}\left(1+\Delta tL_{\text{sun }}\left(t_{k}\right)\right)\right)\left\|e_{0}\right\|+\sum_{j=0}^{N-1}\left(\prod_{k=j+1}^{N-1}\left(1+\Delta tL_{\text{sum }}\left(t_{k}\right)\right)\right)\left\|R_{j}\right\|$$  |
L330: 
L331: This recurrence relation forms the basis for analyzing the stability of the Euler discretization in subsequent steps, which implies the Lipschitz bound:
L332:  | $\displaystyle\|e_{n+1}\|$  | $\displaystyle\leq\|e_{n}\|+\left\|b_{\mathrm{cg}}(a(t_{n}),t_{n})-b_{\mathrm{cg}}(a_{n},t_{n})\right\|\Delta t+\|R_{n}\|$  |
L333:  |  | $\displaystyle\leq\|e_{n}\|+L_{\mathrm{sum}}(t_{n})\,\|a(t_{n})-a_{n}\|\,\Delta t+\|R_{n}\|$  |
L334:  |  | $\displaystyle=\|e_{n}\|+\Delta t\,L_{\mathrm{sum}}(t_{n})\,\|e_{n}\|+\|R_{n}\|$  |
