L346: ### 5.2 Performance Difference
L347: Moreover, we aim to quantify how the introduction of causal guidance affects the behavior and performance of the diffusion policy. To this end, we establish a theoretical guarantee characterizing the performance difference between the causality-guided policy and the base diffusion policy using the Performance Difference Lemma (PDL). The PDL expresses the difference in performance between two policies in terms of the expectation of the advantage function (cite98†Kakade and Langford, 2002 ).
L348: Specifically, for any initial state $s_{0}$, the performance gap between two policies $\pi$ and $\pi^{\prime}$ is given by
L349:  | $$V^{\pi}\left(s_{0}\right)-V^{\pi^{\prime}}\left(s_{0}\right)=\frac{1}{1-\gamma}\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d_{s_{0}}^{\pi}}\left[\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi(\cdot\mid s)}A^{\pi^{\prime}}(s,a)\right],$$  |
L350: where $V^{\pi}\left(s_{0}\right)=\mathbb{E}\left[\sum_{i=0}^{\infty}\gamma^{i}r\left(s_{i},a_{i}\right)\mid s_{0},\pi\right]$ is the discounted return (value function) of policy $\pi$, and $A^{\pi^{\prime}}(s,a)=Q^{\pi^{\prime}}(s,a)-V^{\pi^{\prime}}(s)$ is the advantage function under policy $\pi^{\prime}$ with transition probability $d=p\left(s_{t+1}\mid s_{t},a_{t}\right)$. Thus, we construct our Theorem cite99†1 below.
L351: ###### Theorem 1.
L352: 
L353: Let $\pi$ be the base (unguided) diffusion policy and $\pi_{cg}$ be the causality-guided diffusion policy obtained by adding drift terms with interventions
L354: 
L355:  | $$\gamma_{t}\,\nabla_{a_{t}}\log p_{\varphi}(\cdot\mid\operatorname{\mathbf{do}}(a_{t}))+\beta_{t}\,\nabla_{a_{t}}\log p_{\omega}(\cdot\mid\operatorname{\mathbf{do}}(a_{t}))$$  |
L356: 
L357: Assume the advantage function $A^{\pi}(s,a)$ is finite, then the performance difference satisfies the bound
L358:  |  | $\displaystyle\big|J(\pi_{cg})-J(\pi)\big|$  |
L359:  |  | $\displaystyle\leq\frac{1}{1-\gamma}\,\sqrt{\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{cg}}}\!\big[\sup_{a}|A^{\pi}(s,a)|^{2}\big]}\cdot\sqrt{\tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{cg}}}\!\big[\mathrm{KL}\!\big(\pi_{cg}(\cdot\mid s)\,\|\,\pi(\cdot\mid s)\big)\big]}$  |
L360:  |  | $\displaystyle=\frac{1}{1-\gamma}\,\sqrt{\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{cg}}}\!\big[\sup_{a}|A^{\pi}(s,a)|^{2}\big]}\cdot\sqrt{\tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathbb{E}_{\pi_{cg}}\!\left[\int_{0}^{T}\Big\|\frac{\gamma_{t}\nabla_{a_{t}}\log p_{\varphi}(\cdot\mid\operatorname{\mathbf{do}}(a_{t}))+\beta_{t}\nabla_{a_{t}}\log p_{\omega}(\cdot\mid\operatorname{\mathbf{do}}(a_{t}))}{g(t)}\Big\|^{2}dt\right]}$  |
L361: which implies that
L362: 
L363:  | $$\left|J\left(\pi_{cg}\right)-J(\pi)\right|\leq\mathcal{O}\left(\sqrt{\mathrm{KL}\left(\pi_{cg}\|\pi\right)}\right)$$  |
L364: ###### Proof of Theorem 1.
L365: 
L366: By the Performance Difference Lemma (PDL), we can first have
L367: 
L368:  | $$J(\pi_{cg})-J(\pi)=\frac{1}{1-\gamma}\,\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{cg}}}\Big[\,\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi_{cg}(\cdot\mid s)}\big[A^{\pi}(s,a)\big]\,\Big]$$  |
L369: 
L370: where $d^{\pi_{cg}}$ denotes the (discounted) state-visitation distribution under $\pi_{cg}$, which implies that
L371:  | $$\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi_{cg}}[A^{\pi}(s,a)]=\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi_{cg}}[A^{\pi}(s,a)]-\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi}[A^{\pi}(s,a)]=\mathbb{E}_{a}\!\big[A^{\pi}(s,a)\,(\pi_{cg}(a\mid s)-\pi(a\mid s))\big]$$  |
L372: 
L373: as $A^{\pi}(s,a)=Q^{\pi}(s,a)-V^{\pi}(s)$ and $V^{\pi}(s)=\mathbb{E}_{a\sim\pi}Q^{\pi}(s,a)$. Hence, this PDL can be represented by the factorized expectation:
L374:  | $$J(\pi_{cg})-J(\pi)=\frac{1}{1-\gamma}\,\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{cg}}}\!\Big[\mathbb{E}_{a}\!\big[A^{\pi}(s,a)\,(\pi_{cg}-\pi)(a\mid s)\big]\Big]$$  |
L375: 
L376: For each state $s$, we can derive the upper bound of this expectation:
L377: 
L378:  | $$\big|\mathbb{E}_{a}\!\big[A^{\pi}(s,a)\,(\pi_{cg}-\pi)(a\mid s)\big]\big|\leq\sup_{a}|A^{\pi}(s,a)|\cdot\int|\,\pi_{cg}(a\mid s)-\pi(a\mid s)\,|\,da$$  |
L379: 
L380: The integral on the right is twice the total variation (TV) distance:
L381:  | $$\mathrm{TV}\!\big(\pi_{cg}(\cdot\mid s),\pi(\cdot\mid s)\big)=\tfrac{1}{2}\int|\pi_{cg}-\pi|(a\mid s)\,da$$  |
L382: 
L383: Hence, we can have
L384: 
L385:  | $$\big|\mathbb{E}_{a}\!\big[A^{\pi}(s,a)\,(\pi_{cg}-\pi)(a\mid s)\big]\big|\leq 2\,\sup_{a}|A^{\pi}(s,a)|\cdot\mathrm{TV}\!\big(\pi_{cg}(\cdot\mid s),\pi(\cdot\mid s)\big)$$  |
L386: 
L387: By Pinsker’s inequality, for two probability measures $p$ and $q$, the total variation (TV) distance is bounded by the square root of half Kullback–Leibler (KL) divergence as:
L388:  | $$\mathrm{TV}(p,q)\leq\sqrt{\tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathrm{KL}(p\|q)}$$  |
L389: 
L390: Applying this to the conditional policies at each $s$, we can drive that:
L391: 
L392:  | $$\big|\mathbb{E}_{a}\!\big[A^{\pi}(s,a)\,(\pi_{cg}-\pi)(a\mid s)\big]\big|\leq 2\,\sup_{a}|A^{\pi}(s,a)|\cdot\sqrt{\tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathrm{KL}\!\big(\pi_{cg}(\cdot\mid s)\,\|\,\pi(\cdot\mid s)\big)}$$  |
L393: 
L394: which yields
L395:  | $\displaystyle\big|J(\pi_{cg})-J(\pi)\big|$  | $\displaystyle\leq\frac{1}{1-\gamma}\,\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{cg}}}\!\Big[\,2\sup_{a}|A^{\pi}(s,a)|\,\sqrt{\tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathrm{KL}\!\big(\pi_{cg}(\cdot\mid s)\,\|\,\pi(\cdot\mid s)\big)}\Big]$  |
L396:  |  | $\displaystyle=\frac{2}{1-\gamma}\,\mathbb{E}_{s\sim d^{\pi_{cg}}}\!\Big[\sup_{a}|A^{\pi}(s,a)|\,\sqrt{\tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathrm{KL}\!\big(\pi_{cg}(\cdot\mid s)\,\|\,\pi(\cdot\mid s)\big)}\Big]$  |

